VIDEO(4)                       P32W Unix                        VIDEO(4)

NAME
       video - the video card's chip, for a program's own drawing

SYNOPSIS
       /dev/video   character device 7, minor 0

       #include <sys/video.h>
       write(fd, words, 2 * n)        n command words (unsigned short)
       read(fd, words, 2 * n)         words a command read back
       ioctl(fd, VIOCSYNC, 0)
       ioctl(fd, VIOCINFO, &vi)       struct vidinfo vi

DESCRIPTION
       The video card draws its picture, 640 by 480 dots of black
       or white, with an HD63484 ACRTC. The picture is in the chip's
       own memory: nothing of it can be read or written as bytes.
       The chip draws from commands, 16-bit words: a command's word
       and then its parameters. /dev/video hands them to it as they
       are written, in order, and waits where the chip is busy. So
       a count must be even, and a command may be written in
       pieces.

       While a program has /dev/video open the screen is its own.
       The console (tty4) draws nothing: what is written to it, and
       what the kernel says, is kept and not shown. The screen
       itself is left as it was when the device is opened, with the
       console's text on it: a program that wants it black clears
       it (EXAMPLES).

       When the device is closed, or the program ends or is
       killed, the console has the screen again: the command the
       chip had in hand is stopped, its drawing registers and the
       font are set up afresh, and the text is drawn again as it
       would be if nothing had been drawn, with what was written to
       the console meanwhile. A program can therefore do no lasting
       harm, whatever it writes.

       One program at a time: a second open fails with EBUSY. Only
       root and group staff may open the device.

       The screen: x 0, y 0 is the bottom-left dot, and y runs up:
       the top line is y 479. In the chip's memory a line is 40
       words, a word's bit 0 its left-hand dot; line n from the top
       starts at word n * 40. Under the screen, from y -1 down, are
       eight pictures
       of the font, each 256 dots wide and 48 lines high: 0 plain,
       + 1 inverse, + 2 underlined, + 4 bold. Character c (space to
       ~) of picture a has its top-left dot at
              x = (c & 31) * 8
              y = -1 - 48 * a - 16 * ((c - 32) >> 5)
       and is 8 dots wide and 16 high. VIOCINFO gives these
       numbers, so that a program need not hold them.

       Reading: read() gives the words that VC_RD and VC_RPR
       commands have put in the chip's read FIFO, which holds
       eight: read them before asking for more. It waits for as
       many as are asked for, and returns fewer (or 0) once the
       chip has done everything written and has no more.

       VIOCSYNC waits until the chip has drawn everything written
       so far. VIOCINFO fills a struct vidinfo: width, height,
       memw (words a line), cellw, cellh, fonts, fonty, fontstep.

COMMANDS
       <sys/video.h> names the common ones; the chip's manual has
       them all. Coordinates are 16-bit, a negative one as its
       two's complement.

       VC_AMOVE x y     the current point; VC_RMOVE dx dy
       VC_DOT           the dot at the current point
       VC_ALINE x y     a line from the current point up to x, y.
                        That last dot is not drawn, so that lines
                        joined end to end share no dot; the current
                        point is then x, y. VC_RLINE dx dy
       VC_ARCT x y      a rectangle's four lines, the current point
                        one corner and x, y the other; VC_RRCT
       VC_AFRCT x y     the same, filled; VC_RFRCT
       VC_APLL n ...    lines through n points; VC_APLG closes them
       VC_AGCPY x y dx dy
                        the dx + 1 by -dy + 1 dots whose top-left
                        is x, y, copied to the current point, which
                        ends dx + 1 dots to the right: with the
                        numbers above, a character
       VC_WPR|r v       drawing register r: 0 and 1 the two colours
                        (0 and 0xFFFF as a program finds them), 4
                        a mask, 0x0C and 0x0D the read / write
                        pointer: word a of the memory is written as
                        0x4000 | a >> 12 and (a << 4) & 0xFFFF
       VC_CLR v w l     w + 1 words by -l + 1 lines of memory set
                        to v, from the pointer down
       VC_RD            the word at the pointer, for read(); the
                        pointer moves on a word. VC_WT v writes one

       A drawing command takes an operation in its low bits:
       VO_REPLACE (0), VO_OR, VO_AND or VO_XOR. With VA_INSIDE
       added it draws only the dots inside a rectangle: registers
       8 to 0x0B have its least x, least y, greatest x and
       greatest y.

EXAMPLES
       /usr/examples/draw.c
              lines, boxes, a filled block and text

       unsigned short c[] = { VC_WPR | 0x0C, 0x4000, VC_WPR | 0x0D,
           0, VC_CLR, 0, 39, -479 };
       write(fd, c, sizeof c);
              the screen black

ERRORS
       ENXIO   no video card
       EBUSY   another program has the device; or VIOCSYNC while
               the chip waits for its read FIFO to be read
       EINVAL  an odd count
       EINTR   a signal while waiting for the chip

FILES
       /dev/video, /dev/tty4

SEE ALSO
       gfx(3): a library of lines, circles and text over this
       device

       docs/video-card-acrtc.md, Hitachi's HD63484 ACRTC User's
       Manual

LIMITATIONS
       The emulators' chip has no circles, ellipses, arcs or PAINT:
       such a command does nothing there and is named on the
       emulator's own error output. There is no mouse yet.
